 Maine may not see  anything amiss about the flashing red  and blue lights at the police cars those  familiar with the region would know that  Maine law enforcement vehicles only have  blue lights making the red a dead  giveaway that we're in a different place  than we think then at the end of it  chapter 2 when all is set right by the  adult losers the camera pans over the  town of Derry which is back to its  idyllic Maine setting and free of the  threat of child eating monsters however  there's still one problem a sign for the  exclusively Canadian real estate company  Royal LePage is visible on one of the  buildings which gives away the fact that  this isn't Maine at all or even the  United States the character of Stanley  URIs played by Wyatt Olaf is the son of  the town rabbi who we meet in it chapter  one preparing Stan for his upcoming Bar  Mitzvah in one scene after a frustrated  Stan finishes reading from the Torah he  shuts the book and walks off but when he  closes the book we can see that it is  upside down whoever placed the book in  front of Stan on the set either didn't  know or forgot that Hebrew is read from  right to left  not left-to-right like English which  means that the letters on the cover  should have been to Stan's right not his  left at least when Stan later returns  the book to the shelf in his father's  office he corrects this error and places  it on the Shelf right-side up  when young beverly played by sophia  Lillis runs into the rest of the losers  Club in the pharmacy in it chapter 1  she's trying to figure out which tampons  to buy while the boys are gathering  first aid supplies in order to treat  Ben's injuries after his run-in with  Henry Bowers everyone is incredibly  awkward with each other especially  Beverly since she wants to keep her  period a secret from the boys during  this scene we see a number of Olay  skincare products on a shelf near the  kids the only problem is in 1989 the  brand was called Oil of Olay  it didn't get rebranded into Olay until  1999 you can also see dial and Ivory  soap sporting their modern-day packaging  in the pharmacy scenes along with  Covidien curity bandages which didn't go  by that name until 2007 in it chapter  one Eddie's mother Sonya played by Molly  Atkinson is an enthusiastic consumer of  the snack Swedes brand  mrs. fresh Lee's delicious deals from  oatmeal cream pie cookies to iced honey  buns these treats dominate Eddie's  pantry and when the losers are over at  Eddie's house he tells them to leave  those alone take everything but the  delicious deal guys  the only problem is mrs. fresh Lee's  brand wasn't established until 1994 a  good four years after it chapter one  takes place in addition the packaging on  Sonja's snacks is the same design you  can find in stores today when we meet  Beverly Marsh in it chapter 1 the poor  girl is getting bullied from all sides  her  mother is abusive and the girls at  school torments her mercilessly shaming  her because she kissed a boy in the  school play  and teasing her because she's poor  eventually Beverly tries to hide from  the Mean Girls in the bathroom  but her plan backfires when the girls  find her they dump a bunch of wet  garbage into her toilet stall  fortunately Beverly has her backpack  which she uses to block most of the gunk  raining down on her but just moments  later when she runs into Ben outside her  backpack is totally clean and totally  dry the losers Club aren't only battling  a vicious clown creature they also have  a group of human enemies in the form of  sadistic bully Henry Bowers played by  Nicholas Hamilton and his gang one day  Bowers and his crew of goons chase  homeschooled orphan Mike Hanlon played  by chosen Jacobs into the Barrens where  the losers hang out and a rock fight  ensues this is the first time the rest  of the losers meet Mike who makes their  lucky number seven but an unfortunate  continuity error occurs when Henry's  friend belch begins bleeding before  Beverly's Rock actually makes contact  with his head what's more the rock  catches him on the side of his baseball  cap not across the bridge of his nose  where the injury mysteriously appears  Ritchie trashmouth Tozier played by Finn  wolf Hardin is a kid who's constantly  making snarky quips and his friends are  constantly asking him to shut his  piehole throughout both hit movies many  of Ritchie's comments come in the form  of pointed insults such as the rock  fight between the losers and Henry  Bowers gang when Ritchie yells but while  the term mullet was first coined in 1967  School Hand Luke it didn't actually  enter popular usage until after the 1994  Beastie Boys song mullet egg since the  first installment of it takes place in  1989 Ritchie's comment has no context  for that time period even though it does  for modern-day viewers after the losers  Club think they've defeated Pennywise at  the end of it chapter one  bill still isn't 100% convinced that the  ordeal is over  he asks everyone to swear that if  Pennywise is still alive they'll all  return to Derry to help him defeat it if  it isn't dead  if it ever comes back  we'll come back to to seal their vow to  one another bill uses a broken piece of  glass to cut everybody's palms so that  they can make a blood oath promising  that they'll all come together again if  they need to while this is a powerful  and somber moment symbolizing the  transition of the losers from childhood  to adulthood it is slightly marred by  another continuity error although the  shard of glass bill uses to cut the  losers hands has blood on it after the  first few cuts the blood miraculously  disappears after he cuts Mike's hand and  stays strangely clean for the rest of  the ritual although it chapter one takes  place entirely in 1989 chapter two flips  between 1989 and 2019 as the losers Club  returns dairy to end the battle between  the town and Pennywise once and for all  one of the flashbacks to 1989 features a  key scene at 